  belongs to and is sought after by those  who we look at as being  morally depraved on the wrong track  can that really be a genuine good  and so you know here's where seneca  considers a counter example he says  your premise is false which premise  in the teaching of literature medicine  and navigation we see that the relevant  goods belong to the humblest kinds of  people  now you know teaching of literature you  might say well wait a second what do you  mean a humblest kind of person  well a lot of times the people who were  the tudors  were slaves in ancient rome very often  brought from greece  or medicine doctors were not highly  regarded  i mean they weren't totally at the  bottom of society but they  they didn't have the status that we gave  them now or navigation  you know teaching people how to do this  he says these these skills don't make  any claim to greatness of spirit they  don't sort of the heights  they don't despise the things of fortune  virtue is different  virtue elevates a person and places him  above  all that mortals hold dear and you know  he gives some example about  people who would do you know  things that that are considered to be  disgusting  and he says that there are those whom  wealth befalls in the same way as silver  coins  fall in the sewer virtue takes its stand  above such things it's assessed in its  own  currency things that can belong to just  anybody don't  count with it as goods medicine and  navigation don't debar themselves and  their practitioner from the admiration  of such things  someone who's not a good man can still  be a doctor a helmsman a teaching  teacher of literature and he says for  heaven's sake even a cook  right but you can't be a good person  without having virtue so if we think  about the things that can be  shared if the stoics are right then that  means that if people that we  we look down on or say well they're not  they're not good people  can enjoy that thing it's not truly a  good  even though we might enjoy it too it's  just not  a good in the true sense  there's a really interesting argument  that he provides  after that and here he starts talking  about causes  he says that which is good does not come  of what is bad but wealth comes of  avarice therefore wealth is not a good  so for those who don't know what is  avarice another word for it is greed  wealth comes from this desire to have  wealth  right you desire wealth therefore you  you work to get it or you scheme or you  do whatever you're going to do  invent the proverbial better mouse trap  right  and then you get the money and  so seneca is saying here  that wealth is coming from something  that is bad so wealth cannot be  something good  he says one might say it's not true that  good does not arise from what is bad  because money arises from temple robbery  and theft and he says thus temple  robbing and theft are indeed bad but  only because they produce more bad  consequences than good  for they yield of profit but they also  produce fear anxiety torments  of mind and body and then he says this  is a terrible argument  terrible counter-argument whoever says  this must admit that just as temple  robbery is bad  insofar as it produces bad consequences  it's also good because it produces a  good consequence for you and we don't  want to say  that sort of thing by the way temple  robbery why is this a big deal  oh because it's pissing off the gods no  no it has nothing to do with that  temples were where people  left money it was like a bank so this is  sort of like  robbing the bank being a bank robber is  not a  good thing um  so he goes on and he says goods cannot  arise  from what is actually bad and then he he  has another thing that he says it's  quite interesting  the greatest penalty for criminal acts  is in the acts themselves  now that is paradoxical is it not to say  that those who do the wrong thing  the biggest punishment is that they  actually do the wrong thing because  why is that a punishment they want to do  the wrong thing don't they they're  getting to do what they like and they're  getting away with it in many cases  he says you're mistaken if you make them  wait for the prisoner or the executioner  such acts are punished right away when  performed indeed in the very act of  performing them  therefore good does not arise from what  is bad any more than a fig  from an olive tree goods cannot but  run true to type he says  now he says that some other members of  his school  answer this objection as follows  so here's entertaining the possibility  that money could be good  suppose that money is a good regardless  of where it's obtained  even so and even if money is sometimes  gained by temple robbing it's not the  case that the money actually came from  one's robbing the temple  think about it like this a gold coin is  in the same jar with a snake  if you take out the the coin you did not  take it out by reason of the snake being  there as well  that is to say the jar does not supply  me with gold because it also contains a  snake  it supplies gold in spite of the fact  that it contains a snake in the same way  temple robbing produces a profit not  because it's a despicable and criminal  act  but because it contains profit as well  so you can have the the good  and the bad is just kind of accidental  to it and then he says  these two cases are not the same at all  in the one i can take out the gold  without the stake and the other i cannot  have the prophet without  robbing the temple the prophet is not  adjacent to the crime  but combined with it so when we desire  wealth for you know the wrong reasons  you know there's no getting around  that aspect of it he also talks about  results and he's got an argument here  about results he says  if the pursuit of something brings many  bad results that thing is not a good  our pursuit of wealth brings many bad  results therefore  wealth is not a good and he says  okay somebody might question this one  your premise has two meanings one  meaning is that our pursuit of wealth  brings many bad results  but let's take a counter example  our pursuit of virtue also brings many  bad results  a person takes ship for reasons of study  and is shipwrecked  or captured right they wanted to be  virtuous but it brought about some  bad results then he says the second  meaning is like this  that through which we get bad results is  not a good he says  from a pr such a premise we can't infer  that we get bad results through wealth  or through pleasure or else if we get  many bad results through wealth and  wealth is not only a good but actually a  bad thing  but your school says only that wealth is  not a good not that it's a bad  thing besides you grant that wealth has  its uses  you count it as one of the advantages of  life and so he says well here's a  response to that  you're wrong to think that wealth is a  disadvantage wealth does not harm anyone  what does the harm is one's own  foolishness  or the iniquity of another just as a  sword doesn't harm anyone but only the  tool  is only the tool of the slayer so wealth  doesn't hurt you because you're harmed  for the sake of your wealth and he says  here's a better response and this comes  from the stoic philosopher posidonius  he says wealth is a cause of evils  not because wealth itself does anything  bad itself  right the dollar bills don't do anything  or the stock certificates but it  motivates those who will  do bad things and he talks about here a  difference between  what he calls an efficient cause and an  antecedent cause and here we're getting  into a little bit of stoic metaphysics  that we don't have to go too deep into  suffice it to say an efficient cause is  something that  makes something happen an antecedent  cause  is something that's there in the chain  of causality but it doesn't directly  make the thing happen rather it sets  things up in such a way  that something can happen so he says  here  good things or wealth has this latter  sort of causality it inflames the mind  gives birth to arrogance attracts  resentment and sets the mind at odds  with itself  to such an extent we delight to have a  reputation for affluence even though  their reputation is likely to hurt us  so the wealth by itself doesn't do  anything  but it brings out the worst stuff in us  attributable to us by the way not  actually attributable to the wealth  and he says listen good things are pure  they they don't corrupt the mind they  don't cause anxiety  they elevate and expand the mind right  and he says somebody might go too far  and say wealth is not only a good  not only not a good thing but is even a  bad thing and  seneca says no no because it doesn't  harm us in itself  it doesn't have efficient causality but  we do have to be  careful with wealth because of that and  then it goes on and talks about  some other arguments that posidonius is  giving and there's actually two  syllogisms here  posidonia says that you should reason  like this those things which confer on  the mind neither greatness nor  confidence nor freedom from anxiety  are not goods that's one premise wealth  health and similar things do not do any  of these things  second premise therefore they're not  goods and then he expands the  syllogism saying those things which  confer on the mind neither greatness nor  confidence nor freedom from anxiety but  instead produce  extravagance conceit and arrogance are  bad  things dependent on fortune drive us to  these ladder therefore they are not  goods  now notice he's not saying that that by  this wealth would become something bad  but it's definitely not a good and the  idea here  is that you know we the the greatest  values are these things  uh greatness of mind confidence freedom  from anxiety  if you can't actually provide that then  it's not really a good  and he seneca goes on and says one might  say by this reasoning there  will not even be advantages or what the  stoics would call  preferred indifference and he says  advantages are of a different status  from goods an advantage is that in which  there's more utility  than an annoyance a good has to be a  genuine good  and innocent of any sort of harm so a  good is on a higher level  than something that is merely  advantageous something that's  useful something that is convenient  the last syllogism that seneca  is going to provide here  runs like this and and this is a little  tricky one  it says that which is good is not made  up of things that are bad  but many instances of poverty make up  one instance of wealth  therefore wealth is not a good let's  contextualize this a little bit so  something that's good is not going to be  made up of a whole bunch of instances of  something that's bad  what would be being poor okay so if i  only have ten dollars in my pocket  i'm pretty poor at least here in the  united states right  if that's all i have to subsist on i'm  you know  i got maybe one or two meals ahead of me  and that's about it  depending on where i'm living now  what if i multiply that by a thousand  and i've got ten thousand dollars that's  actually a lot of money  it's not that much money it's not like i  could live on that for a year  unless i'm being extremely frugal so  let's uh add another  thousand of that and now we've got 10  million oh now i'm really rich right  that 10 million is made up of a whole  bunch of little ten  dollars and each of those ten dollars is  a little bit of poverty  so how do you combine all this poverty  and make wealth well you do so by  engaging in  sophistical reasoning right and seneca  says this is not a good argument  this is this syllogism is not recognized  by our school  it's both devised and solved by the  peripatetics the aristotelians  posidonius however says this sophism  which has been bandied about by  dialecticians is refuted by  auntie potter who is one of the stoic  skull arcs  by saying this and here's a good  argument  poverty refers not to possession but to  subtraction or as the ancients call the  privation  it refers not to what someone has but  what they don't have  thus it's not possible for anything to  be filled up from many  instances of emptiness wealth is made up  of numerous resources not numerous  instances of scarcity you don't properly  understand what poverty is poverty is  not the condition of possessing a few  things  but that of not possessing many things  being deprived of many things  its meaning is not derived from what it  has but what it lacks  and so you know this is a very  interesting metaphysical resolution  and you notice seneca is willing to  reject that argument and and that says  something  seneca only wants to use arguments that  he thinks are actually on point with  this  and he's going to be very critical about  how these arguments are used  here's the question we want to close  with that seneca is is kind of closing  with him  himself how effective are these  syllogisms do they actually  convince people do they change their  mind do they help them see  that wealth itself is not the good that  so many people and our culture and the  culture of seneca's time  present it as being that we need to  instead  shift our focus to something that's  genuinely good  like virtue  you
 

